// Research Program page (A3g §7). Explains how Xiaochuan works in general; it must
// not repeat the whole modality case, which lives on Current Work (/research).
---

<Base
  title="Research Program · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="A research program on how structured meaning is represented, processed, and learned, connecting formal semantics with psycholinguistic and computational evidence."
>
  <article class="rp">
    <h1 class="rp-h1">Research Program</h1>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">From meaning to a computational account</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        My work asks how language shapes the possibilities we keep available and revise as
        new information arrives. My current focus is epistemic modality: what expressions
        such as <em>might</em> contribute, how children understand them, and how these
        distinctions can be made explicit in a simple cognitive model.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The next direction is to use such a model to specify representations, update rules,
        and predictions, then ask whether corresponding computations can be identified in
        a language model. The cognitive model and this correspondence are still to be established.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">From linguistic analysis to model tests</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <strong>Linguistic analysis</strong> states what expressions contribute and which
        inferences they license. It supplies contrasts and competing explanations; a semantic
        description alone does not specify a psychological algorithm.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <strong>Cognitive modelling</strong> makes a candidate explanation runnable: what
        information is represented, how it changes, and what answers or errors should follow.
        Alternative models need to make distinguishable predictions.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <strong>Human studies</strong> test those predictions against people's understanding
        and reasoning. The planned thesis studies preschool children; its measures and contrasts
        must suit that population. It has no language-model component.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <strong>LM mechanism tests</strong> ask whether changing a candidate internal state
        produces the selective changes predicted by the cognitive model. Reading a variable
        from an activation is a starting point; an intervention must do more than change an answer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">What would count as a correspondence?</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        If changing a variable in the simple model predicts a particular pattern of changed and
        unchanged answers, an aligned intervention in the LM should produce the same pattern
        on new examples. The alignment could be distributed across many units and hold only
        within a limited task. Even a successful alignment would not establish that people
        use the same algorithm; that requires independent human evidence.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v236/geiger24a.html">Distributed alignment search</a>
        and <a href="https://jmlr.org/papers/v26/23-0058.html">causal abstraction</a> provide
        methodological starting points. Whether they support a useful explanation of the modal
        phenomenon is an open research question.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Where the projects fit</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        <a href="/research/three-ways">Three Ways of Leaving <em>p</em> Unsettled</a> develops
        linguistic contrasts. The <a href="/research/learnability">planned master's thesis</a>
        provides the route to evidence from children. A separate
        <a href="/research/modal-language-models">model project</a> is the intended home for
        a simple computational account and its comparison with LMs; diagnostic runs exist,
        while cognitive modelling, controlled training, and internal alignment tests remain ahead.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The
        <a href="/research/typicality-in-referent-choice">typicality working paper</a> remains
        a separate piece of linguistic analysis.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">A programme, with separate studies</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        These projects can constrain one another without sharing every manipulation, dataset,
        or completion date. A mismatch may reveal a weak analysis, an inadequate cognitive model,
        a measurement problem, or a genuinely different computation. The aim is to distinguish
        those explanations, not to assume a common mechanism in advance.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="rp-foot"><a href="/research">See how this program is being developed in the current work →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

